# .cursorrules

## Project Context
Quiz Game Platform - Plataforma educativa gamificada en tiempo real


## Component Guidelines
- Always use shadcn/ui components when available
- Use Tailwind 4 utility classes only (no arbitrary values unless necessary)
- Follow React 18 best practices (useTransition, Suspense, Concurrent Features)
- Prefer named exports for components
- Always include proper TypeScript types (strict mode)

## Styling Rules
- Mobile-first responsive design
- Use CSS variables from shadcn theme (hsl color system)
- Animations: Framer Motion for complex, CSS for simple
- Follow BEM naming for custom classes (rare cases)
- Dark mode support with CSS variables

## shadcn/ui Components Available
button, card, dialog, dropdown-menu, toast, progress, tabs, 
sheet, tooltip, avatar, badge, scroll-area, separator, 
skeleton, slider, switch, textarea, input, label, select,
alert, alert-dialog, form, checkbox, radio-group

## Naming Conventions
- Components: PascalCase (GameCard.tsx)
- Files: kebab-case for utilities (audio-manager.ts)
- Hooks: camelCase with 'use' prefix (useGameState.ts)
- Stores: camelCase with Store suffix (gameStore.ts)
- Types: PascalCase (GameStatus, UserRole)
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE

## Code Style
- Use const/let, never var
- Prefer arrow functions
- Use optional chaining (?.) and nullish coalescing (??)
- Destructure props and objects
- Use template literals for strings
- Avoid deep nesting (max 3 levels)

## TypeScript
- strict: true
- noImplicitAny: true
- strictNullChecks: true
- Never use 'any' (use 'unknown' if needed)
- Always type function parameters and return types
- Use interfaces for objects, types for unions/primitives
- Use enums sparingly, prefer union types

## React Patterns
- Functional components only
- Custom hooks for reusable logic
- Context + useReducer for complex state (or Zustand)
- React.memo for expensive renders
- Lazy load routes and heavy components
- Suspense with fallbacks
- Error boundaries for error handling

## Performance
- Code split with React.lazy
- Virtualize long lists (@tanstack/react-virtual)
- Debounce/throttle expensive operations
- Memoize expensive calculations (useMemo)
- Optimize re-renders (useCallback, React.memo)
- Lazy load images (react-lazy-load-image-component)

## API & Data Fetching
- Use TanStack Query for server state
- Use Zustand for client state
- Optimistic updates where appropriate
- Handle loading and error states
- Cache strategies: staleTime, cacheTime
- Invalidate queries on mutations

## Forms & Validation
- React Hook Form for forms
- Zod for validation (shared schemas with backend)
- shadcn/ui Form components
- Client-side validation first
- Server-side validation always

## Real-time (WebSockets)
- Socket.IO client
- Auto-reconnect on disconnect
- Handle connection states (connecting, connected, disconnected)
- Namespaces for separation (/game, /chat)
- Rooms for specific sessions

## Audio System
- Use Howler.js for all audio
- Separate volume controls (sfx, music)
- Preload critical sounds
- Mute option
- Sound sprites for efficiency

## Animations
- **Framer Motion**: Page transitions, layout animations, complex interactions
- **GSAP**: Particle effects, timelines, high-performance animations
- **Lottie**: Static animations (icons, badges, common pets)
- **Rive**: Interactive animations (rare/epic pets with state machines)
- **CSS**: Simple hover effects, loading spinners
- 60fps target, use transform/opacity for performance

## Security
- Sanitize user input
- Validate on both client and server
- CORS configured properly
- Rate limiting on API endpoints
- JWT tokens with refresh strategy
- HttpOnly cookies for sensitive data
- Content Security Policy headers

## Accessibility
- Semantic HTML
- ARIA labels where needed
- Keyboard navigation support
- Focus management
- Color contrast ratios (WCAG AA)
- Screen reader friendly

## Error Handling
- Try/catch for async operations
- Error boundaries for React errors
- User-friendly error messages
- Log errors to Sentry (production)
- Toast notifications for user feedback
- Fallback UI for errors

## Testing
- Vitest for unit tests
- React Testing Library for component tests
- Playwright for E2E tests
- Test user flows, not implementation
- Mock external dependencies
- Aim for 70%+ backend coverage, 60%+ frontend

## Git Workflow
- Feature branches (feature/name)
- Conventional commits (feat:, fix:, docs:, etc.)
- Pull requests for code review
- No commits to main directly

## Do NOT
- Use inline styles
- Create custom components for things shadcn already has
- Use 'any' type
- Ignore TypeScript errors
- Make API calls in components (use hooks/queries)
- Store sensitive data in localStorage
- Use var
- Create global variables
- Use dangerouslySetInnerHTML without sanitization
- Commit .env files
- Push node_modules
- Use console.log in production (use proper logging)

## Prisma Guidelines
- Use transactions for related operations
- Index frequently queried fields
- Use select to minimize data transfer
- Soft delete (deleted_at) instead of hard delete
- Use proper relations and constraints
- Handle Prisma errors gracefully



## Deployment
- Railway/Render for backend
- Vercel/Netlify for frontend
- Environment variables for config
- GitHub Actions for CI/CD
- Staging environment for testing

## Documentation
- JSDoc for complex functions
- README for setup instructions
- API documentation (endpoints, request/response)
- Component documentation (props, usage examples)
- Update docs when changing functionality

## Pet System Specifics
- Common pets: CSS Sprites
- Uncommon pets: Lottie animations
- Rare/Epic pets: Rive (state machines)
- Legendary pets: Three.js (optional 3D)
- Renderer based on rarity
- Smooth evolution transitions

## Game Modes
- Classic: Kahoot-style, simultaneous answers
- Board: Snakes & ladders progression
- Survival: Elimination mode
- Boss Battle: Multi-phase epic encounters

## Economy
- Coins (soft currency): Daily limit 5000
- Gems (hard currency): No daily limit
- Event Tokens (temporary)
- Balance sources vs sinks
- Transaction logging for audit

## Comments
- Explain WHY, not WHAT
- Document complex algorithms
- TODO/FIXME for future work
- Keep comments up to date

